<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Yaka Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Lunda-founded kingdom on the Kwango river, ruled by the Kiamfu, which dominated the Yaka until Belgian conquest in 1906.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> warriors from the east, moving out from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a>, conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaka-people">Yaka</a> and other peoples of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwango-river">Kwango</a> valley. Their leader took the title Kiamfu and founded a kingdom centred at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tippu-tips-state">Kasongo</a> Lunda.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Kiamfu ruled through regional chiefs, many of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> descent, who paid tribute in slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raffia-cloth">raffia cloth</a>. The kingdom acted as a middleman between the interior and Portuguese-linked traders in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kasanje">Kasanje</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaka-people">Yaka</a> initiation rites and masks became widely known in African art.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State's</a> forces fought the Kiamfu repeatedly in the 1890s and established control by 1906. The Kiamfu title survives as a customary office in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1700 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.53%;width:4.10%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Yaka religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kiyaka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, <a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kiamfu, Kwango Yaka, Lunda of the Kwango</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1803&amp;focus=yaka-kingdom" aria-label="Yaka Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5321 2245 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yaka Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5662 2807h0M5362 2652h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5641 2501h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5641 2501h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Democratic Republic of the Congo. <a href="/atlas?year=1803&amp;focus=yaka-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

